const topic = $('Fetch Topic-Specific Research Sources').item.json.topic;
const excludeList = (topic.excludeTerms || []).join(', ');
const schema = {worth_it: true, topic_match: true, confidence: 0.0, reason: '', failure_mode: 'headline_is_the_whole_tip|too_obvious_already_known|not_actually_non_obvious|no_concrete_payoff|needs_too_much_setup|off_topic|null'};
const prompt = `You are the pre-production judge for a 10-15 second developer-tips video series about ${topic.displayName}. This show covers ONLY ${topic.scope}${excludeList ? ` Explicitly excluded: ${excludeList}.` : ''}

Your job has two parts. FIRST, confirm the idea below genuinely belongs to that scope -- set topic_match to false and worth_it to false and failure_mode to "off_topic" if it does not, regardless of how good the idea otherwise is. SECOND, only if it is in scope: decide whether a developer who has ALREADY read the full idea below -- the hook, what it is, why it's non-obvious, and the payoff, exactly as a caption or thumbnail would reveal it before they tap play -- would still choose to watch the 10-15 second video instead of scrolling past.

This is a harsh bar. Most "tips" fail it because the caption already told the viewer everything of value: knowing the headline IS knowing the tip, so the video adds nothing. A tip clears the bar only when watching still buys the viewer something the headline alone can't: seeing exactly where the option lives, seeing the before/after, hearing the one nuance that prevents misuse, or confirming this isn't something they already do. Judge specifically from the perspective of an experienced developer who has seen a thousand "did you know" posts and skips almost all of them.

Score worth_it as true only if a developer would keep watching after already knowing the full idea, AND topic_match is true. Set failure_mode to the single best reason when worth_it is false, or null when true.

IDEA UNDER REVIEW:
${JSON.stringify({tool_name: candidate.tool_name, topic: candidate.topic, category: candidate.category, hook: candidate.hook, what_it_is: candidate.what_it_is, why_not_obvious: candidate.why_not_obvious, concrete_payoff: candidate.concrete_payoff, who_it_helps: candidate.who_it_helps})}

Return ONLY valid JSON matching this schema:
${JSON.stringify(schema)}`;
return [{json: {...$json, candidate, prompt}}];

